# The composed remainder worked out through the parts, beside the one layers/remainder.sqlc takes
# from the composed totals. One row per composed layer that owes an exact remainder.
#
# Not the composed nameplate less the composed demand
#   The composed remainder is the parts' own remainders, converted, less the nameplate elimination
#   and plus the demand elimination, and the point is that it is not the composed nameplate less
#   the composed demand. One conversion factor multiplies both a part's nameplate and its demand,
#   so the two converted totals move together, and subtracting them takes that factor's range in
#   twice. Converting each part's own remainder takes the factor in once per term.
#   composition/settled_remainders.sqlc is that conversion, one row per settled layer with the
#   path's factors already multiplied in; this is the sum over it plus the correction.
#
# Both eliminations appear, with opposite signs
#   Removing double-counted demand raises the remainder; removing double-counted nameplate lowers
#   it. A sentence that is evaluated nowhere can lose a whole term without anything noticing, and
#   this relation is what evaluates it.
#
# Which corner an elimination takes
#   An elimination is counted in one quantity, so it moves with that quantity: the nameplate
#   elimination is at the nameplate's low where the nameplate is low, the demand elimination at the
#   demand's high where the demand is high. Which end of its own range that is, the quantity's sum
#   rule has already decided: bound by bound it is low with low, and where the sum turned over and
#   took the crossed pairing it is low with high. eliminations/paired.sqlc decides which, for the
#   eliminations filed here and, through composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc, for those at the
#   layers a walk passes, so `n_at_low`, `n_at_high`, `d_at_low` and `d_at_high` are each
#   elimination read at its own quantity's corners. Then the corner of the remainder decides which
#   end of each quantity is in play:
#
#   - where no factor is a range, the demand drives it: the remainder falls as the demand rises,
#     so the lowest remainder takes the nameplate at its low and the demand at its high;
#   - where a factor is a range and the remainder is above zero, nameplate and demand both scale
#     with the factor, so the remainder rises with it, and the lowest remainder takes both
#     quantities at their low.
#
#   With no factor a range, this makes the remainder exactly the composed nameplate less the
#   composed demand, each end against the opposite end, wherever the composed layer's filed totals
#   are their sums.
#
# The sign of the sum does not decide it
#   It only looks as though it does. It agrees with what drives the remainder on every layer whose
#   remainder stays on one side of zero, and disagrees where the remainder runs from short to
#   spare, because there the sign changes between the two corners while the driver does not.
#   `every-local-part/both-views` is that layer: two identical parts and eliminations equal to one
#   whole copy of each, so removing one copy from two must leave exactly one copy. Read by the sign
#   its highest value comes out 600 too large, which is the demand elimination's own width.
#
# Four layers that tell the rules apart
#   The figure each must land on:
#   - merge-group-composition/labour, driven by the demand and short: -3.0, -1.5 and -0.4;
#   - merge-holding-composition/compute, driven by the factor and spare: 1414.0, 2857.0 and
#     4085.6;
#   - every-local-part/both-views, driven by the demand, from short to spare: -240, 160 and 360;
#   - every-inverting-elimination/shift-capacity, its nameplate crossed: 0, 4 and 7.
#
# One read of each elimination relation, turned into columns
#   Both quantities' eliminations come from one copy of eliminations/paired.sqlc and one of
#   composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc, each keyed by quantity and turned into columns with
#   `FILTER`. A join per quantity would ask the same relation twice and plan it twice, and it sits
#   under every rule that reads a remainder.
#
# The zero fill is right only inside the owed population
#   The `coalesce(..., 0)` is right only because composition/owed_remainder.sqlc is joined first.
#   Inside that population an absent elimination means the composer looked and found no double
#   counting, which is the number zero. Outside it the same blank means nobody looked, and filling
#   it would put a claim in the check that the document declined to make. The population leaves out
#   a layer whose walk passes a layer whose own sum is suspended, which keeps the carried terms
#   numbers too.
#
# A filed remainder that adds the demand elimination straight
#   Where a filed remainder adds the demand elimination straight, it sits one elimination's width
#   below this figure, which is the right rule for an addition applied to a figure that runs from
#   short to spare. queries/observations/13-composed-remainder.sqlc prints this relation beside the
#   one from the totals. Two figures in one document cannot both be right where an elimination is
#   a range, and which of them moves is the filer's call rather than this relation's.
#
# The two eliminations are not independent
#   Each is read at the corner of the remainder through the quantity it was counted in, so the two
#   eliminations move with the same scenario. Read as independent of each other, `both-views` comes
#   out at a low of -840: a week in which the double-counted demand is at its least while the
#   double-counted nameplate is at its most, which the filing's own figures do not allow. That
#   independent reading is kept for one case only: where the paired reading turns over, it is the
#   widest reading in order the terms allow, and the remainder falls back to it.
-- composition/settled_remainders.sqlc summed over the settled frontier, less
-- eliminations/paired.sqlc at their own corners.
SELECT y.composition, y.composed_layer,
       CASE WHEN y.paired_low <= y.pivoted_mode AND y.pivoted_mode <= y.paired_high
            THEN y.paired_low  ELSE y.r_low  - y.n_high + y.d_low  END AS pivoted_low,
       y.pivoted_mode,
       CASE WHEN y.paired_low <= y.pivoted_mode AND y.pivoted_mode <= y.paired_high
            THEN y.paired_high ELSE y.r_high - y.n_low  + y.d_high END AS pivoted_high,
       y.derived_low, y.derived_mode, y.derived_high, y.derived_fit, y.unit, y.parts, y.spread
FROM (
    SELECT x.*,
           x.r_low  - x.n_at_low
             + CASE WHEN x.spread AND x.r_low >= 0 THEN x.d_at_low  ELSE x.d_at_high END AS paired_low,
           x.r_mode - x.n_mode + x.d_mode                                            AS pivoted_mode,
           x.r_high - x.n_at_high
             + CASE WHEN x.spread AND x.r_low >= 0 THEN x.d_at_high ELSE x.d_at_low  END AS paired_high,
           least(x.n_at_low, x.n_at_high)    AS n_low,
           greatest(x.n_at_low, x.n_at_high) AS n_high,
           least(x.d_at_low, x.d_at_high)    AS d_low,
           greatest(x.d_at_low, x.d_at_high) AS d_high
    FROM (
        SELECT c.composition, c.composed_layer,
               sum(c.r_low)  AS r_low,
               sum(c.r_mode) AS r_mode,
               sum(c.r_high) AS r_high,
               bool_or(c.spread_factor) AS spread,
               coalesce(max(e.n_at_low),  0) + coalesce(max(k.n_low),  0) AS n_at_low,
               coalesce(max(e.n_mode),    0) + coalesce(max(k.n_mode), 0) AS n_mode,
               coalesce(max(e.n_at_high), 0) + coalesce(max(k.n_high), 0) AS n_at_high,
               coalesce(max(e.d_at_low),  0) + coalesce(max(k.d_low),  0) AS d_at_low,
               coalesce(max(e.d_mode),    0) + coalesce(max(k.d_mode), 0) AS d_mode,
               coalesce(max(e.d_at_high), 0) + coalesce(max(k.d_high), 0) AS d_at_high,
               max(d.r_low)  AS derived_low,
               max(d.r_mode) AS derived_mode,
               max(d.r_high) AS derived_high,
               max(d.derived_fit) AS derived_fit,
               max(d.unit)   AS unit,
               count(*)      AS parts
        FROM      (
            :compose(composition/settled_remainders.sqlc)
        ) c
        JOIN      (
            :compose(composition/owed_remainder.sqlc)
        ) o  ON o.filing = c.composition AND o.layer = c.composed_layer
        JOIN      (
            :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
        ) d  ON d.filing = c.composition AND d.layer = c.composed_layer
        LEFT JOIN (
            SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer,
                   max(p.at_low)  FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_at_low,
                   max(p.mode)    FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_mode,
                   max(p.at_high) FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_at_high,
                   max(p.at_low)  FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_at_low,
                   max(p.mode)    FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_mode,
                   max(p.at_high) FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_at_high
            FROM (
                :compose(eliminations/paired.sqlc)
            ) p
            GROUP BY p.composition, p.composed_layer
        ) e ON e.composition = c.composition AND e.composed_layer = c.composed_layer
        LEFT JOIN (
            SELECT w.composition, w.composed_layer,
                   max(w.e_low)  FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_low,
                   max(w.e_mode) FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_mode,
                   max(w.e_high) FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_high,
                   max(w.e_low)  FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_low,
                   max(w.e_mode) FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_mode,
                   max(w.e_high) FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_high
            FROM (
                :compose(composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc)
            ) w
            GROUP BY w.composition, w.composed_layer
        ) k ON k.composition = c.composition AND k.composed_layer = c.composed_layer
        GROUP BY c.composition, c.composed_layer
    ) x
) y
